# cartridge.conf
#
# Put this file at the root of the cartridge. It is the only file a cartridge
# needs — there are no launch scripts to write and nothing to allowlist.
#
# When the drive is plugged in, the launcher reads this, shows the cover art and
# the title, and waits. Nothing runs until you press Play.
#
# There are two formats: single-game (simple key=value) and multi-game bundle
# (uses [collection] + [game] sections).
#
# ===== Single-game format =====
#
# ----- Keys -----
#
# executable  (required)
#   What Play should start. Either:
#     - a URI the OS already knows how to handle:
#       steam:// heroic:// gog:// epic:// playnite:// lutris:// http:// https://
#     - a path to a file on the cartridge, relative to this one
#       (Game\bin\MyGame.exe on Windows, Game/bin/start.sh on Linux)
#
# title  (optional)
#   The name shown in the launcher. Defaults to "Unknown Game".
#
# cover  (optional)
#   Cover art, relative to this file. PNG, JPG, WEBP, AVIF or GIF.
#   Portrait art at 3:4 fills the window exactly; anything else is centre-cropped.
#   Must stay on the cartridge — ..\ paths are refused — and is capped at 8 MB.
#   If omitted, the launcher looks for cover.png, cover.jpg, poster.png or box.png
#   at the root.
#
# save  (optional, repeatable)
#   Where this game keeps its saves on the host, so they can travel with the
#   cartridge. Written as
#
#     save=Label|{token}/rest/of/the/path
#
#   The label is optional; without one the last part of the path names it.
#   Repeat the key for a game that keeps saves in more than one place.
#
#   The {token} is required and is the point: a save path cannot be written
#   down literally, because the three platforms disagree about where it goes.
#   Each token resolves on the host that reads it:
#
#     {home}          the user's home directory
#     {documents}     Documents (the localised name, on Linux)
#     {savedgames}    Saved Games
#     {appdata}       Windows %APPDATA%    · macOS ~/Library/Application Support
#                     · Linux $XDG_CONFIG_HOME (~/.config)
#     {localappdata}  Windows %LOCALAPPDATA% · macOS ~/Library/Application Support
#                     · Linux $XDG_DATA_HOME (~/.local/share)
#     {appsupport}    as {localappdata}, named for the macOS case
#     {prefs}         macOS ~/Library/Preferences · %APPDATA% · ~/.config
#
#   A path that resolves to a whole home, Documents or AppData directory is
#   refused: a save declaration has to name one game's saves inside one of
#   them, not the directory itself.
#
#   Where one platform genuinely differs — a Windows game whose saves are
#   inside a Proton prefix, say — write a platform line as well. Where both
#   exist, the platform one wins on that platform and the plain one covers the
#   rest:
#
#     save=Saves|{appdata}/Foo/Saves
#     save.linux=Saves|{home}/.steam/steam/steamapps/compatdata/1/pfx/Foo
#
#   Syncing is off until the user turns it on. When it is on, the two copies
#   are compared against what this machine last saw of each; only the side
#   that changed is copied, both changing is a conflict that writes nothing,
#   and whatever is about to be replaced is moved aside and kept first.
#
# portable_home  (optional, cartridge-wide)
#   yes to give a game the cartridge *carries* its own home directory, at
#   .gamepak/home/ on the drive. Every save it writes lands there, and no
#   save= line is needed for it at all.
#
#   This is the better mechanism where it applies, because nothing has to be
#   researched or declared and it cannot miss a path the game uses. It applies
#   only to a game stored on the cartridge and started by the launcher itself:
#   a game the cartridge points at with a steam:// URI is started by Steam, in
#   Steam's environment, which nothing here can set.
#
#   Off unless asked for. Changing where a game thinks home is can stop it
#   working — a game that expects its own settings will find none and start at
#   its defaults — and only the person making the cartridge knows whether
#   theirs minds.
#
#   Set on Linux: HOME and the XDG_*_HOME directories. On macOS: HOME, which
#   ~/Library follows. On Windows: USERPROFILE, APPDATA and LOCALAPPDATA.
#   The cache is deliberately left on the host: it is rebuildable, it is the
#   largest thing a game writes, and a shader cache on removable flash costs
#   write cycles for nothing.
#
#   One caveat, and it is the filesystem's: exFAT cannot hold a symlink, so a
#   game that makes one inside its config directory will fail to. btrfs and
#   NTFS cartridges have no such problem.
#
# savemode  (optional, cartridge-wide)
#   copy (the default) or link. `link` replaces the host's save directory with
#   a symlink onto the cartridge, so there is only ever one copy. It is faster
#   and exact, and it costs two things: Windows refuses symlinks to an
#   unprivileged process unless Developer Mode is on, and a drive pulled
#   without ejecting leaves the game's save directory dangling. Use copy
#   unless you have a reason.
#
# ----- Example -----

executable=steam://rungameid/1091500
title=Cyberpunk 2077
cover=cover.jpg
save=Saves|{documents}/CD Projekt Red/Cyberpunk 2077

# ----- A carried game, which needs no save line at all -----
#
# executable=Games/Tunic/start.sh
# title=Tunic
# portable_home=yes

# ----- More examples -----
#
# Heroic (GOG / Epic)
#   executable=heroic://launch/gog/1207658921
#
# A game that ships on the cartridge itself (Windows)
#   executable=Game\bin\MyGame.exe
#
# A native Linux game on the cartridge
#   executable=Game/bin/start.sh
#
#
# ===== Multi-game bundle format =====
#
# A [collection] section defines the overall cartridge metadata (required).
# Each [game] section defines one game (at least two needed for bundle mode).
#
# [collection] keys:
#   title  - Collection name shown in the launcher (required)
#   cover  - Collection cover art, relative to this file (optional)
#
# [game] keys:
#   title       - Game name shown in the game list (required)
#   executable  - What Play should start for this game (required)
#   cover       - Per-game thumbnail, relative to this file (optional)
#   save        - Where that game keeps its saves (optional, repeatable).
#                 Same form as above; a save line inside a [game] section
#                 belongs to that game.
#
# ----- Bundle example -----
#
# [collection]
# title=God of War Collection
# cover=collection.jpg
#
# [game]
# title=God of War (2018)
# executable=steam://rungameid/310970
# cover=cover_0.jpg
#
# [game]
# title=God of War Ragnarök
# executable=steam://rungameid/2322010
# cover=cover_1.jpg
# save=Ragnarök|{savedgames}/God of War Ragnarok
#
# Steam games are stored under steamapps/ as usual.
# GOG / other games are stored under Games/<Title>/ for organisation.
